# THE CHANGELOG IS NOT MERGED WITH `union`, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED.
#
# It was, from the day the contract was written, on this reasoning: the file is
# append-at-the-top, so two branches that each ship an entry insert at the same
# line and collide every time; union keeps BOTH insertions instead of raising a
# conflict, and the stamper then assigns the numbers.
#
# The stated hazard was "two branches editing the SAME EXISTING entry", called
# rare, and the everyday prepend was called safe. That is backwards. **The
# prepend is the case that corrupts**, because union is a LINE union and an
# entry is not a line: when both sides prepend, the shared closing `    ] },`
# is common context and survives ONCE, so the first entry swallows the second
# and the literal is left with an unclosed bracket. The result is still valid
# JavaScript — `node --check` passes it — so nothing downstream notices until a
# human reads the file.
#
# Measured 2026-08-15: this happened on FIVE consecutive merges in one day
# (#126, #132, #136, #139, R-BUG4), each needing the same manual repair —
# rebuild from the base copy and re-stamp. Union did not save one conflict; it
# converted five loud conflicts into five silent corruptions that had to be
# repaired by hand anyway.
#
# So the changelog merges normally now. Two branches that both ship an entry
# CONFLICT, loudly, at the moment of the merge, and the resolution is the
# obvious one: keep both entries, newest first, and re-run
# `node chicago/4d/tools/stamp-changelog.mjs`.
#
# The old comment also claimed "the contract check catches that — versions must
# be strictly decreasing". That check DOES exist (`check-changelog.mjs`, the
# `entries must be newest-first by v` rule) and always has. What it could not do
# is REPORT: it lives after the module load, and the shape walk above it calls
# report() and exits the moment a bracket is unbalanced. The merge that creates
# a duplicate version is the same merge that breaks the shape, so the run always
# died on the shape first and the duplicate was never named — then the hand
# repair rebuilt the file from a base copy, taking the duplicate with it. The
# check was right and unreachable in exactly the case it was written for.
# It is now ALSO enforced in the text scan, which runs before that exit, so a
# duplicate is reported even when the literal will not load.
#
# See polecat-platform docs/SHELL-API.md § the fleet changelog contract. The
# fleet's other apps carry the same `merge=union` line and the same exposure.

# QUEUE.md IS MERGED BY A QUEUE-AWARE DRIVER, AND NOT BY `union`.
#
# The lesson above applies here and points the other way. Union is a LINE union;
# it wrecked the changelog because a changelog ENTRY is many lines. A queue entry
# IS exactly one line, so union would not corrupt this file silently — and
# `ticket.mjs check` catches every union failure mode loudly (`lists X twice`,
# `is not an open ticket`, `missing from QUEUE.md`).
#
# But loud is not resolved. On the case that actually hurts — one side re-ranks
# the file on the owner's instruction while the other closes and files tickets —
# union concatenates BOTH orderings and hands back every ticket twice. Measured
# 2026-09-04: landing one re-rank took four merges of `dev` and four hand
# reconciliations, every one of them the same three-line rule.
#
# So that rule is a driver instead:
#     result = OURS, minus what THEIRS closed, plus what THEIRS filed.
# Ours keeps the ORDER (order is the owner's, and the branch being merged into is
# the one holding his instruction); theirs decides MEMBERSHIP (a closed ticket is
# closed, a filed one is real). It REFUSES rather than guesses if the result would
# carry an id twice.
#
# Register it once per clone:  bash chicago/4d/tools/setup-merge-drivers.sh
# Not registered? git falls back to the ordinary text merge and you get conflict
# markers — the old behaviour, never something worse. `check.sh` says so.
chicago/4d/tickets/QUEUE.md merge=queue

# …AND THE CHANGELOG GETS ONE TOO, for the same reason and with the same care.
#
# Everything above about union stands: it is why `merge=union` is NOT on these
# paths and must not come back. But removing union left a conflict on nearly
# every branch, resolved by hand every time — three times on 2026-09-04 alone,
# each the identical edit. A DRIVER IS NOT UNION: union is line-blind, and this
# one never works below entry granularity, so it cannot splice two entries
# together the way union did.
#
#     result = OURS' new entries (reset to `v: null, ts: ''`) on top of
#              THEIRS' entries, verbatim and in their order.
#
# Theirs is settled history — already on dev, already stamped — so it is taken
# whole and never renumbered. Ours goes on top UNSTAMPED, which is exactly what
# the contract asks authors to write and what stamp-changelog.mjs then assigns:
# two branches that each guessed "top + 1" both get it wrong.
#
# SO THE MERGE IS NOT DONE WHEN THE DRIVER RETURNS. It prints the reminder:
#     node chicago/4d/tools/stamp-changelog.mjs
#     node chicago/4d/tools/check-changelog.mjs
#
# It REFUSES — leaving ordinary conflict markers — if both sides edited the same
# shipped entry differently, or if a title would appear twice. That is precisely
# the case union got silently wrong, and it is rare enough not to automate.
chicago/4d/renderers/web/js/changelog.js merge=changelog
site/chicago/4d/js/changelog.js merge=changelog
site/chicago/4d/walk/js/changelog.js merge=changelog

# THE BUILD PRODUCTS, AND THE ONE FILE IN THIS SET THAT IS NOT ONE (T-0831).
#
# THE MEASUREMENT. PR #906 was open about seventy minutes. `dev` moved FIVE times
# under it — #904, #907, #908, #905, #858 — and every one of the five merges
# conflicted, always in generated files and never once in the substantive diff:
#
#   lap 1  #904   BOARD.md, tickets.json x2, build.json, walk/index.html
#   lap 2  #907   the same five (plus STATUS.md, a real conflict, hand-merged)
#   lap 3   --    the same five, measured with merge-tree before the lap
#   lap 4  #905   the same five
#   lap 5  #858   BOARD.md, tickets.json x2
#
# Two other PRs report it independently. #894: "each one collides on the same four
# generated files" — four rebases paid, and the fifth is where that run's clock ran
# out. #850: "Every conflict so far has been in a generated file... The substantive
# diff has merged cleanly every time" — rebased twice, the gate run three times on
# three bases, then parked on `hold` with finished, green work. #850 prices the lap
# at ~19 minutes of honest verification, during which dev took three more merges.
#
# WHY KEEPING OURS IS SAFE HERE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT. Each of these five is
# ALREADY REFUSED BY THE GATE WHEN STALE — checked by reading check.sh, not assumed:
# BOARD.md and tickets.json by `ticket.mjs check`; the site tickets.json by
# `test_ticket_mirror.mjs`, which asserts that a mirror somebody else made stale
# still fails; build.json and walk/index.html by `check_published.mjs`, which holds
# that every published file is byte-identical to its source or is a declared
# transform. So the conflict was never what protected these files — the gate was,
# and the conflict is pure cost. That is the same reasoning chicago/4d/.gitattributes
# already sets out for the liberty register: a conflict is safe because it stops
# you, and where a check already stands in for it, the conflict buys nothing.
#
# The driver KEEPS OURS AND PRINTS the rebuild command, because a driver that
# silently keeps ours also silently removes the merge's own reminder that the file
# needs rebuilding. merge-changelog.mjs prints its follow-up for the same reason.
#
# It is NOT `merge=union`, which is banned above on measured grounds and must not
# come back here: union is a LINE union and it turned five loud changelog conflicts
# into five silent corruptions in one day. This driver merges no content at all.
#
# Register once per clone:  bash chicago/4d/tools/setup-merge-drivers.sh
# Not registered? git falls back to the ordinary text merge and you get conflict
# markers — the old behaviour, never something worse.
chicago/4d/tickets/BOARD.md      merge=generated
chicago/4d/tickets/tickets.json  merge=generated
site/chicago/4d/tickets.json     merge=generated
site/chicago/4d/build.json       merge=generated
site/chicago/4d/walk/index.html  merge=generated

# …AND THE FILE THAT LOOKS LIKE ONE OF THEM AND IS NOT.
#
# tools/dev-smoke-state.json sits in exactly the same conflict set, and treating it
# the same way would have destroyed data. Measured before writing the rule: it is an
# APPEND-ONLY register of smoke readings (T-0216), 62 of them; its rows carry NO
# `id`, so T-0820's uniqueness check cannot see it; and NO step of check.sh reads it
# at all. Nothing regenerates it and nothing would notice a merge throwing half of
# it away. #905 resolved one lap by taking dev's side and said so — correct for one
# lap, wrong as a standing rule, because a reading is evidence that a gate was run
# on a tree and evidence is not regenerable.
#
# So this one gets a union of WHOLE READINGS — ours in ours' order, then theirs'
# that ours does not already hold, deduplicated on a canonical form that sorts keys
# at every depth. Never a union of lines. It refuses, leaving ordinary conflict
# markers, if either side is not the shape it knows.
chicago/4d/tools/dev-smoke-state.json  merge=smokestate
